# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf` decides — and it is per-machine. A Windows
# checkout with autocrlf=true writes CRLF into every file, a Linux one writes
# LF, and the same commit is two different byte streams depending on who looked.
#
# That is normally invisible. It stopped being invisible when a test read a
# source file and searched it for a pattern written with \n: on the Windows
# checkout the file held \r\n, the search matched nothing, and the test reported
# "no route registered at /settings" for a route that was plainly there. Seven
# tests failed that way, all of them false, and a suite that cries wolf is worse
# than no suite — the next failure gets waved through too.
#
# LF everywhere, then. Git normalises on commit and checks out unchanged, so the
# bytes a test reads are the bytes that were written.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch. A keystore or a PNG with a byte "corrected"
# from 0x0D to 0x0D0A is a keystore or a PNG that no longer works.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.jks binary
*.keystore binary
*.pdf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.m4a binary
*.apk binary
*.aab binary

# Windows scripts genuinely need CRLF; cmd.exe will not run them otherwise.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
